claude-simplify's report holds optional simplification proposals, each with its current and replacement lines, no defect claims: never judge them by the defect standard or put them in the ledger. Keep a proposal when all hold: its current lines match `d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3` and are lines this PR added or modified (in a re-review, lines changed since the previous review), it removes something a reader must hold (a branch, helper, mode, flag, duplicated block, allocation or control-flow trap), not just moves, renames or rephrases code, what it removes or changes is something this PR added, never a construct a line already had before a mechanical edit (a renamed call, a swapped logger), and your own equivalence probe proves it preserves behaviour: a focused test, or a command that runs the old and the new code on the same inputs. Give each probe one line in Checks: the command, marked `(equivalence probe)`, and its result. Drop a proposal without one, and any that edits authorization, sandboxing, escaping, URL checks, deletes, migrations, infrastructure or concurrency code, or code that records money or usage, unless it removes a defect-prone construct. Drop a proposal whose saving the author must re-verify, or that gains one or two lines: an assertion that guards a path its neighbours do not (a serialized form another consumer renders), a condition whose implication is only how one caller builds the value, not the type's contract, a constant folded into a list whose name then no longer fits every member, named steps or one line per item folded into one expression, a shared helper for a job that runs once and is removed, a hot path, a run that doubles as a check, a sourced file, a stub an example asserts on, a name that fits one branch, a value chosen another way without a test. One comment per idea: a proposal becomes a ```suggestion at its first site plus "Same change at L…" for the others. Its first line is the title alone, `**Simplification** (keeps behavior: apply it, or reply with the risk)`, then a blank line, one sentence on what it removes, and the suggestion. Post at most 5, the most substantial, ordered by what they remove, most first. They never affect the verdict. Add `"candidates":{"claude-simplify":{"suggested":N}}` to the result file, N the ones you post.

Read `prepass` first: the removed definitions that files outside the diff still name, the changed source files without a test change, each changed method's body before its hunk, and each new file's closest existing sibling, to read before it. Each item is a lead to check, never a finding by itself.

`evidence` lists what earlier rounds examined in this PR, by path and behaviour: read the areas marked `unknown` or `stale` (changed since) first, as leads, never as findings by themselves; a `probed` entry with `control: true` needs no new probe, as its file is unchanged since.

